# AutoBuild-H5000M-Openwrt feed configuration - mainline OpenWrt.
# NOTE FOR EDITORS: scripts/feeds strips comments with s/#.+$// and then skips
# blank lines. A lone "#" with nothing after it is therefore NOT a comment to
# that parser and dies with "Syntax error in feeds.conf.default". Every comment
# line here must have text after the hash.
# Upstream source is openwrt/openwrt (branch main, i.e. SNAPSHOT). The official
# OpenWrt feed repositories publish their development branch as master, which
# is what openwrt/openwrt's own feeds.conf.default references - keep that so
# the feeds stay in lockstep with the tree we build.
# Since OpenWrt 25.12 the package manager is apk, not opkg; CONFIG_PACKAGE_*
# symbols are unchanged and nothing here is ImmortalWrt-specific.

src-git packages https://github.com/openwrt/packages.git;master
src-git luci https://github.com/openwrt/luci.git;master
src-git routing https://github.com/openwrt/routing.git;master
src-git telephony https://github.com/openwrt/telephony.git;master
# src-git video https://github.com/openwrt/video.git;master
# NOTE FOR EDITORS: the video feed is deliberately NOT listed. It carries 62
# packages - Qt5, SDL2/SDL3, Wayland, Westone, wlroots, Mesa, Vulkan, LLVM,
# Plymouth, WPEWebKit, gzdoom, vice - and this is a headless 5G CPE, so not one
# of them can be installed on it, let alone used.
# Two reasons it has to stay out:
#   1. Build time. Those 62 packages drag in LLVM, Mesa and the whole Qt5
#      toolchain, which is a large slice of a multi-hour build for zero gain.
#   2. Kconfig. Upstream 5c8e736 ("kirkwood: drop kernel 6.12 support") lands
#      right after da6323e5 "build: remove the AUDIO_SUPPORT and
#      DISPLAY_SUPPORT symbols" / de2f69bf "treewide: drop the audio and
#      display target features" / c375123b "kernel: drop the audio and display
#      feature gates". Those commits deleted the build-feature gate that used
#      to keep every display/audio package OUT of the Kconfig graph on a target
#      without those features. With the gate gone, ~140 packages that used to
#      be filtered now emit Kconfig symbols, and along with them ~140 NEW
#      "recursive dependency detected" errors (26 before the drift, 166 after).
#      Kconfig resolves a cycle by DROPPING a symbol, and with the graph that
#      polluted it dropped PACKAGE_qmodem - which cascaded into
#      luci-app-qmodem-next and luci-app-qmodem-generic (they depend on it) and
#      killed the build in verify_config.
#      feeds/video/frameworks/qt5/qt5base/Config.in:18 is the FIRST cycle Kconfig
#      prints (a `choice` whose members gate the very symbol the choice depends
#      on), so the video feed is the single largest contributor.
# If a display-class package is ever wanted here, add the feed back behind a
# switch in write_feeds_conf() the way wwand is - do NOT re-add it
# unconditionally, or the qmodem build breaks again on any upstream tree that
# still carries the removed gate.

# NOTE FOR EDITORS: the wwand feed (ddimension/openwrt-repo) used to live here
# unconditionally. It is now appended by write_feeds_conf() in
# scripts/local-build.sh, only when ENABLE_WWAND=true.
# Why it had to move: the feed carries libubus-lua-async, which declares
# PROVIDES:=libubus-lua together with CONFLICTS:=libubus-lua. package-metadata.pl
# turns that pair into a Kconfig loop:
#     symbol PACKAGE_libubus-lua is selected by PACKAGE_libubus-lua-async
#     symbol PACKAGE_libubus-lua-async depends on PACKAGE_libubus-lua
# and kconfig resolves a loop by dropping a symbol. The symbol it drops takes
# the whole qmodem dependency chain with it, so on a QModem build (where
# ENABLE_WWAND is forced off anyway) defconfig silently discarded qmodem,
# luci-app-qmodem-next and luci-app-qmodem-generic. verify_config() caught it
# and refused to ship firmware, which is how this was found.
# A feed whose packages are wanted must not be dropped; a feed that is not
# wanted must not be installed at all. wwand is the latter whenever QModem or
# luci-app-mt5700m owns the data path, so it is conditional now.
